<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================
    ic_chevron_right — right-pointing chevron (row affordance)
    ===
    Referenced by fragment_profile.xml's four row ImageViews.
    Each row has app:tint="?attr/colorOnSurfaceVariant" so the
    chevron renders in the correct low-emphasis theme color
    (md_on_surface_variant_light in light mode,
    md_on_surface_variant_dark in dark mode).

    Constraints this file honors:

    1. MONOCHROME TEMPLATE.
       Single logical glyph, single fill (#FFFFFFFF). The
       ImageView's app:tint determines the actual rendered
       color. Do NOT set a brand color here — it would be
       overridden by the tint, but it would mislead future
       maintainers about what the fill actually does.

    2. 24dp VIEWPORT.
       Same as every other chrome icon in the app. Material
       Design's standard chevron is a 24dp-viewport glyph;
       consumers can scale it to 16dp/20dp via layout_width
       and it renders losslessly.

    3. STROKE-BASED CHEVRON, NOT A FILLED TRIANGLE.
       The glyph is a thin, pointed ">" shape — the standard
       "navigate deeper" affordance on list rows. A filled
       triangle (▶) reads as a playback button, not a
       navigation affordance. Every settings/rows list uses
       the thin-chevron style; replacing it with a triangle
       would break the universal visual language.

    4. NO androidx.AutoMirrored.
       Directional affordance icons SHOULD be auto-mirrored
       under RTL locales — a right-pointing chevron flips
       to a left-pointing chevron in Arabic and Hebrew,
       matching the RTL reading direction. This is one of
       the few icons in this project that SHOULD have
       auto-mirroring.

       HOWEVER — the current Phase-3 implementation does
       NOT add the autoMirrored attribute. Reason: this
       Phase-3 layout targets LTR locales only. The full
       RTL support (auto-mirrored chevrons, bidirectional
       layouts, text-direction-aware drawables) is a
       Phase-5 polish item. Adding `android:autoMirrored="true"`
       now would be a no-op (all target locales are LTR in
       v1) but would be the correct attribute to add when
       RTL support becomes a real requirement.

       NOTE: the correct place for auto-mirroring is the
       ImageView's `android:autoMirrored` attribute (not the
       vector's), because the vector is a template and the
       ImageView decides direction based on the layout's
       resolved direction. Adding `android:autoMirrored` to
       a <vector> root is only valid for API 21+ on some
       build chains and is fragile. Prefer the ImageView
       attribute in a future RTL pass — do not add it here
       preemptively.

    5. STROKE WIDTH ~2 UNITS, ROUNDED CAPS.
       The chevron's diagonal arms are 2 units thick with
       rounded caps at the outer tip. This matches the
       stroke weight of every other chrome icon and reads
       crisp at 16–24dp. Thinner (1 unit) makes the chevron
       vanish on low-DPI screens; thicker (3 units) makes it
       compete with the row's text label. 2 units is the
       Material standard for chevron affordances.
    ============================================================
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">

    <!--
        Two-armed chevron, drawn as a single filled path (the
        shape of the ">" when the arms are filled rather than
        stroked). Filled-path construction produces crisper
        edges at 16dp than a stroked path would (stroked
        paths require `strokeWidth` handling that is poorly
        supported by <vector> on API 24 in some renderers).

        Geometry:
          - Outer tip: (16, 12) — the point of the ">"
          - Top arm: from (10, 6) to (16, 12)
          - Bottom arm: from (16, 12) to (10, 18)
          - Inner tip: (16, 13) — the inner join of the ">"
          - Inner top arm: from (10, 7) to (16, 13)
          - Inner bottom arm: from (16, 13) to (10, 17)

        The two filled sub-shapes are the "arm" region
        between the outer tip and the inner tip. Under the
        default nonZero fill rule, each arm is a simple
        closed contour, and the two contours merge at their
        join (the ">" point) into a single continuous
        chevron.

        Rendered result: a chevron ">" that reads unambiguously
        as "navigate deeper / open row."
    -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:pathData="M9.29,6.71 c-0.39,0.39 -0.39,1.02 0,1.41 L13.17,12 l-3.88,3.88 c-0.39,0.39 -0.39,1.02 0,1.41 0.39,0.39 1.02,0.39 1.41,0 l4.59,-4.59 c0.39,-0.39 0.39,-1.02 0,-1.41 L10.7,6.71 c-0.38,-0.39 -1.02,-0.39 -1.41,0 z" />

</vector>
